  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nThe 00Z upper air analysis shows the mean westerlies split around\na broad low amplitude ridge over the central Plains, with the\nnorthern stream riding through Canada and the southern stream\nwrapping beneath a closed low over the Great Lakes, leaving the\narea under weak flow aloft. Warm, dry air sits just above the\ninversion at 850 mb, and broad surface high pressure is centered\nover the mid Mississippi Valley region into portions of the\ncentral Plains. Friday ran colder than forecast with highs only in\nthe upper 40s as the stratus held all day, especially over eastern\nzones. Early this morning the stratus deck is still in place\nacross the far eastern zones, while western areas have cleared and\ncooled into the low 30s and areas of fog have formed over central\nand north central Kansas mainly west of the highway 81 corridor.\n\nThe REFS and global ensembles are in generally good agreement on\nthe stratus clearing from west to east through this morning into\nmidday, so the question is when, not whether for this cycle. As\nthe surface high slides east and winds turn southwesterly, mixing\nreaches the warm air aloft and highs should climb to near 60 with\nwestern areas possibly low 60s with fewer clouds to begin with.\n\nFog potential is the focus Sunday morning as skies clear tonight\nand winds go calm, especially in the valleys. RRFS soundings reach\nthe crossover point by daybreak, so patchy fog is favored with the\nbest development around sunrise.\n\nSunday through Wednesday remains quiet and mild. The upper ridge\nbuilds into Tuesday, bringing highs in the upper 60s to near 70\nand lows in the 40s under mostly clear skies for the period.\nEnsemble spread on those highs is minimal and rain chances stay\nnear zero, so confidence in the mild, dry stretch is high.\n\nThe pattern breaks Thursday as a Pacific trough crosses Canada and\ndives through the northern Plains toward the mid Mississippi\nValley and western Great Lakes. Every model suite signals this\ntrough, but they differ on how deep and how far west the trough\naxis digs. The GFS and its ensembles, along with the AI-GEFS,\ncarry the deeper solution with the trough axis farther southwest,\nwhich puts a sharper front and highs stuck in the 50s over the\narea Thursday. The Canadian and ECMWF keep the axis farther east\nand the trough shallower, which would hold the area on the milder\nside with the heart of the cooler airmass displaced further\nnortheast of the area. The NBM sits closer to the milder camp with\nThursday highs in the mid 60s, but the deeper solution has gained\nground over the past day, so the forecast leans cooler than the\nblend for Thursday and Friday with gusty north winds behind the\nfront. Rain chances stay limited either way until the following\nweekend due to the lack of overall moisture in place.",
